                                [Exception Table]
                            'Detection FOV size.\nLarger FOV = wider area scanned by AI.'
                            'Detection confidence threshold.\nHigher = stricter, fewer false detections.'
                            'Assist Strength.\nControls how fast the assist moves towards the target.\nHigher = faster movements.'
                            'Reduces AI thread usage (4 -> 2).\nEnable this if you experience Fortnite FPS drops or stutters.\nRecommended for i5 or older CPUs.'
                            'Shape of the FOV: Circle (default) or Square.\nSquare also captures the full square region instead of masking it down to a circle before the AI sees it.'
                            "Masks out the bottom-left corner of the capture (where your own viewmodel/hands usually sit) so the AI doesn't see or detect it.\nOn by default - turn off if that corner is cutting off something you need detected."
                            "Size of the black masked-out box in the bottom-left corner.\n1 = smallest, 8 = largest, 4 = default size.\nTurn this up if your viewmodel/hands still poke out past the mask, or down if it's covering more of the FOV than it needs to."
                            'AI Processing Rate (Loops per second).\nHigher = smoother tracking but more CPU load.\nLower = fixes FPS drops and game stutters.\nTo Un-Cap: Drag slider to the far right (320+).'
                            "Maximum number of targets the model will report at once.\nOnly affects how many detections come out of a single frame - it doesn't change what the model sees or how confident it is.\nLower = keeps only the top few highest-confidence boxes (slightly less post-processing work).\nHigher = keeps more simultaneous targets when several are visible at once (e.g. multiple enemies on screen)."
                            'NMS (Non-Maximum Suppression) threshold.\nWhen the model draws several overlapping boxes for what\'s really the same target, NMS keeps the highest-confidence one and discards the rest that overlap it too much.\n\nThis value is how much overlap counts as "the same target":\nLower = stricter - boxes only need a little overlap to get merged into one (fewer duplicate/flickering boxes on a single target, but two close-together targets can get merged into one).\nHigher = looser - boxes need to overlap a lot before one gets discarded (better at telling close-together targets apart, but more duplicate boxes can survive on a single target).'
                            'Aim Smoothness\nControls how gradually the crosshair slows down as it approaches the target.\n\nHigher Smooth = softer, more natural deceleration and less snapping.\nLower Smooth = faster, more direct corrections with sharper stops.\n\nThis value affects:\n  Distance-based damping (smooth slowdown near target)\n  How quickly movement transitions from fast → slow\n  Overall stability when close to the target'
                            "WIN32 CUSTOM / DEFAULT WIN32: plain user32 calls.\nCan be switched live, any time, from this menu.\n\nBUILD / RP2350 / RP2040 / MAKCU / KMBOX NET / KMBOX B+ / FERRUM:\nNeed a driver/hardware handshake done once at startup - picking one here that wasn't chosen at startup just prompts a restart.\nWhichever of these WAS chosen at startup stays connected in the background even after switching away, so you can come back to it any time without restarting."
                            'Traces a slow circle with the cursor using the currently active input method, so you can confirm movement is actually working (and how smooth it looks) without needing to be in-game.'
                            'Reads the physical mouse/keyboard plugged into the box.\nOpens a separate window showing the live state.\nIndependent from the connection - toggle anytime, no reconnect needed.'
                            'DX CAM: Default screen capture backend (dxshot).\nHC CAPTURE: DXGI Desktop Duplication (hc.pyd), no extra deps.\nSwitch between them any time, swaps live, no restart needed.'
                            "DEFAULT: Relative smoothing and strength.\nFeels like 'natural' aim assist.\n\nRAGE: Raw pixel-to-sensitivity math for a 1:1 snap.\nNeeds your EXACT in-game XY Sens and Targeting %.\nFill out the sensitivity boxes or it'll fly across the screen or barely move at all."
                            "--- RAGE SMOOTHING ---\nRECOMMENDED: 50% (Default)\n\nLOWER % (25-45): Faster snap. High risk of 'shaking' or jitter. Use for close range.\nHIGHER % (55-100): Slower pull. Looks more human/legit. Better for long range.\n\nWARNING: If you don't know what you're doing, keep this at 50%.\nToo low will make your crosshair vibrate like a crackhead."
                            'Target Height.\nAdjust vertical offset for the assist.\nHigher = Head\nLower = Lower on body.'
                            'Trigger FOV size in pixels.\nDetermines area to check for locked targets.\nPrimarily for autoshoot, confidence auto-set to 0.85.'
                            'Allows the assist to work with a controller.\nNote: Plug controller in before launching.'
                            'Enable Lead Prediction:\nAims slightly ahead of moving targets to compensate for movement.'
                            "Enable ADS Settings:\nUse separate Confidence, Strength, and Smoothing values specifically for when you're aiming down sights (Aiming Keybind held - Keybinds tab -> IN-GAME, Right Mouse Button by default). Falls back to the normal values otherwise.\n\nRequires your Main Aim Key / Secondary Key (Keybinds tab) to be something other than the Aiming Keybind. ADS detection works by checking whether the Aiming Keybind is held, separately from whatever key activates the assist - if that key is the SAME as the Aiming Keybind, the assist can only ever be active while ADS also reads as 'held', so it would always use the ADS values above and the normal ones would never be reached. A banner appears at the top of this tab if that's currently the case."
                            'Detection confidence threshold while ADS (Aiming Keybind) is held.'
                            'Assist Strength while ADS (Aiming Keybind) is held.'
                            'Assist Smoothing while ADS (Aiming Keybind) is held.'
                            'Prediction strength:\nHigher values increase the lead distance applied to moving targets.'
                            'Enable Weapon Slots:\nActivate per-weapon FOV settings linked to your in-game slot keybinds.\nGo to the Keybinds tab to assign keys and adjust FOV for each slot.'
                            'Optimization Mode:\nUltra-Performance — Maximum speed and lowest latency. Precision is significantly reduced at distance.\nPerformance — High-speed setting optimized for close-range tracking.\nBalanced — Standard competitive setting for all-around use.\nAccuracy — Higher precision and improved aim stability.\nExtreme-Accuracy — Ultimate precision and stickier lock-on. Best for long-range and sniping.'
                            'Bone Targeting:\nHead / Neck / Chest — Aim at a fixed point on the enemy hitbox.\nCustom — Choose your own aim height.\nTarget point adjusts automatically for distance.'
                            "Show a green dot predicting where the target will move.\nNote: 'Show Detections' must be enabled for this to work."
                            'Displays detected objects/targets on screen.'
                            'Opens a separate window with debug information.'
                            'Slightly see-through menu (95% opacity) when on. Fully solid when off.'
                            'Automatically fires when a target is detected.'
                            'Only activates when the configured keybind is held.'
